;; =======================================================
;; COLLISION (BLITZ MODE)
;; =======================================================
;; Player tile collision, ported from the NTSC cartridge. The ROM has no
;; box: it probes fixed points selected by player size and crouch, read out
;; of Block_Buffer_1/2, and decides what to do from the metatile number it
;; finds there. Those two ideas -- probe points, and a metatile class that
;; carries behaviour -- are what this file implements.
;;
;; GEOMETRY. The box is 12x14, inset 2 px on the left, right and top and
;; flush at the bottom, sampled at five points: the top edge at its centre,
;; the bottom edge at both ends, and each side edge at its own extreme.
;; The ROM reaches these numbers through a 32-px OAM envelope that small
;; Mario's art sits at the bottom of, so head Y+18 and foot Y+32 are really
;; Y+2 and Y+16 in this game's 16 px sprite: the geometry is identical, only the arithmetic is rebased.
;; See the main project's PLAYER_HITBOX note section 4.2 and the constants block in
;; sparkydave.constants.ab3.
;;
;; GROUND SUPPORT. There is no separate ground pass. CheckPlayerFeet runs
;; every frame and re-derives grounded-ness from the foot probes, which are
;; an OR: one foot is enough. The ROM forces the player into the falling
;; state at the top of PlayerBGCollision and lets InitSteP put him back on
;; the ground, so he is briefly airborne by construction. Because that
;; re-derivation happens after the vertical move, a player standing still
;; never dips -- matching the ROM, which also applies gravity against the
;; previous frame's state. The old separate CheckPlayerGround pass required
;; both feet and held the player up for up to 16 px of travel after he walked
;; off a ledge. That was this engine's own construction, not a simplification
;; of anything in the ROM.
;;
;; SNAPPING. Only the foot test snaps, to the tile row it landed on. A
;; ceiling hit does not move the player at all: the ROM only changes the
;; vertical speed, and 1 px/tick downward is enough to leave the tile. A
;; wall hit pushes exactly 1 px rather than snapping to the tile face, so
;; ImpedePlayerMove's argument is signed and a wall is where his 12-px box
;; finally rests, flush against the face. Because nothing divides a
;; possibly-negative playerX by #TILE any more, the left-edge signed
;; truncation hazard is gone.
;;
;; METATILE CLASSES come from the generated metatileBuffer and are the only
;; thing this file knows about level content. The #MT_* codes are generated
;; by tools/build_assets.py alongside the buffer, so there is exactly one
;; place they are defined.
;;
;; NOT YET PORTED. Per-direction solidity: the ROM lets the upper of a pipe top's two side probes pass.
;; The exception is not in the code yet.
;;
;; The blocked-input mask (the main project's PLAYER_HITBOX note section 4.8). ImpedePlayerMove{} writes playerCollisionBits and
;; ApplyCollisionButtonMask{} ANDs it into the button read. The mapping is checked only for the side-hit path: the foot-hit path
;; reuses this routine with the opposite push sign, which the cartridge passes as Player_MovingDir (ChkFootMTile, 12012-12014), and
;; it is not known whether that is intended or a bug in the cartridge; this code copies it as it is.
;;
;; See the main project's LEVEL_FORMAT note for the level data and
;; the main project's PLAYER_HITBOX note for the full audit with ROM line numbers.

;; Past the area's own last column (areaColumns) is solid ground, as before its first; mapWidth is the map buffer's row stride
;; (#MAP_W, the widest area's), not this area's width.
Function.w MetatileAt{pixelX.w, pixelY.w}
  SHARED metatileBuffer(), mapWidth, areaColumns
  If pixelY < 0 Then Function Return #MT_EMPTY
  tileColumn.w = pixelX/#TILE
  tileRow.w = pixelY/#TILE
  If tileRow >= #MAP_H Then Function Return #MT_EMPTY
  If tileColumn < 0 Then Function Return #MT_GROUND
  If tileColumn >= areaColumns Then Function Return #MT_GROUND
  Function Return metatileBuffer(tileRow*mapWidth + tileColumn)
End Function

;; The map as the enemies and the items meet it: the same, and a jumpspring's two tile rows (16 across, 32 down from its y) are solid. In
;; the cartridge the spring is two solid metatiles ($67 over $68, drawn over by its sprites), so a Goomba or a Koopa walking into it
;; turns round and one falling onto it stands on it. Ours is an object, so its box is looked up in the area's spring table (a level
;; has none or one: the loop is empty everywhere else). Mario does not use this: he has UpdateSpring{}.
Function.w EnemyMetatileAt{pixelX.w, pixelY.w}
  SHARED springSpawnCount, springSpawnX(), springSpawnY()
  For springIndex = 0 To springSpawnCount - 1
    If pixelX >= springSpawnX(springIndex) AND pixelX < springSpawnX(springIndex) + #TILE
      If pixelY >= springSpawnY(springIndex) AND pixelY < springSpawnY(springIndex) + #TILE*2 Then Function Return #MT_BLOCK
    EndIf
  Next
  Function Return MetatileAt{pixelX, pixelY}
End Function

Statement ImpedePlayerMove{pushPixels.w}
  SHARED playerX, playerSpeedX, sideCollisionTimer, playerCollisionBits
  ;; ExIPM bail (smbdis 12327/12331->12347): the ROM skips the position
  ;; push, the speed zero and the timer when the push would not reverse the
  ;; current motion -- i.e. when the push goes the same direction the player
  ;; is already moving. That is the "player overlapping a wall while moving
  ;; away from it" case, which the NES treats as a separation that should not
  ;; drag the player backward. On bail the bit mask still runs, matching the
  ;; ExIPM fall-through that always executes the EOR/AND on $00.
  pushBails.w = False
  If pushPixels > 0
    If playerSpeedX > 0 Then pushBails = True
  Else
    If playerSpeedX < 0 Then pushBails = True
  EndIf
  If pushBails = False
    ;; A blocked direction zeroes the whole-pixel speed and leaves the subpixel
    ;; alone, like ImpedePlayerMove, which writes Player_X_Speed only.
    playerX = playerX + pushPixels
    If playerX < 0 Then playerX = 0
    playerSpeedX = 0
    sideCollisionTimer = #MARIO_SIDECOLLIDE
  EndIf
  ;; Mask the blocked direction out of the NEXT frame's input. pushPixels < 0 is
  ;; a right-wall stop -- pushed left -- and clears RIGHT; the other way round
  ;; clears LEFT. That is the ROM's side-hit mapping: DoPlayerSideCheck passes $02
  ;; as the side selector (12031), ImpedePlayerMove eor #$ffs it and ANDs the
  ;; result into Player_CollisionBits (12345-12349), and $fd clears d1, the right
  ;; button. That $02 is the ROM's selector -- #MARIO_BTN_RIGHT is $01 here -- so
  ;; this engine writes $fe for the same event. See the main project's PLAYER_HITBOX note
  ;; section 4.8.
  ;;
  ;; This runs on both the push path and the ExIPM bail path: the ROM's
  ;; ExIPM label falls through to the same EOR/AND pair (12347-12350), so a
  ;; bailed wall still blocks the direction that led the player into it.
  ;;
  ;; Assigned, not ANDed in: the byte is then a function of this collision alone, not of last frame's byte.
  ;;
  ;; $ff and not $ffff: only the low byte is read. Xor() is the bitwise form (Amiblitz3.guide 3.1.4); NOT is
  ;; avoided because the guide calls it logic NOT (3.1.3).
  If pushPixels < 0
    playerCollisionBits = Xor($ff, #MARIO_BTN_RIGHT)
  Else
    playerCollisionBits = Xor($ff, #MARIO_BTN_LEFT)
  EndIf
End Statement

;; A big Mario who is not crouching: the one case with the big head probe and the two upper side
;; probes (ChkCollSize, smbdis 11929); a small or a crouching Mario has the small set. One
;; place for the rule, used by the head probe, the side probes and the box.
Function.w BigStanding{}
  SHARED playerIsBig, playerCrouching
  standingBig.w = False
  If playerIsBig = True
    If playerCrouching = False Then standingBig = True
  EndIf
  Function Return standingBig
End Function

Statement CheckPlayerFeet{}
  SHARED stompChain, playerOnGround, playerX, playerY, playerSpeedY
  SHARED playerMoveForceY, playerFractionY, playerMovingDirection
  If playerSpeedY < 0 Then Statement Return
  ;; Either foot is enough. The ROM checks the left probe, and only if it is
  ;; clear does it read the right one (DoFootCheck, 11988-11990).
  If MetatileAt{playerX + #PROBE_FOOT_X1, playerY + #PROBE_FOOT_Y} = #MT_EMPTY
    If MetatileAt{playerX + #PROBE_FOOT_X2, playerY + #PROBE_FOOT_Y} = #MT_EMPTY
      playerOnGround = False
      Statement Return
    EndIf
  EndIf
  ;; More than 4 px into the tile row is not a landing, it is an obstruction
  ;; in the way of a moving player, and the ROM impedes horizontal motion
  ;; instead (cpy #$05 / bcc LandPlyr, 12009-12011). This cannot happen on flat
  ;; ground: the worst fall step is #MARIO_FALL_MAX + 1 pixels, and a landing is allowed at the
  ;; #MT_FEET_MIN_SUBTILE residues 0 up to that number minus one, so as long as the step is no
  ;; more than #MT_FEET_MIN_SUBTILE a fall cannot skip all of them (NTSC 5 and 5, PAL 6 and 6).
  ;; tools/check_source_split.py checks it for both variants.
  If (playerY AND $0f) >= #MT_FEET_MIN_SUBTILE
    If playerMovingDirection = #MARIO_BTN_RIGHT
      ImpedePlayerMove{-1}
    Else
      ImpedePlayerMove{1}
    EndIf
    Statement Return
  EndIf
  footRow.w = (playerY + #PROBE_FOOT_Y)/#TILE
  playerY = footRow*#TILE - #TILE
  playerSpeedY = 0
  playerMoveForceY = 0
  playerFractionY = 0
  playerOnGround = True
  stompChain = 0
End Statement

Statement CheckPlayerSides{}
  SHARED playerX, playerY, playerSpeedX
  If playerX < 0 Then playerX = 0
  rightSideBlocked.w = False
  leftSideBlocked.w = False
  If MetatileAt{playerX + #PROBE_SIDE_X2, playerY + #PROBE_SIDE_Y} <> #MT_EMPTY
    rightSideBlocked = True
  EndIf
  If MetatileAt{playerX + #PROBE_SIDE_X1, playerY + #PROBE_SIDE_Y} <> #MT_EMPTY
    leftSideBlocked = True
  EndIf
  ;; A big Mario standing has two more probes, 16 px higher (the ROM's four side probes, smbdis
  ;; 12012 and the main project's PLAYER_HITBOX note 4.2); a small or crouching one has the lower pair only.
  ;; Standing up under a ceiling puts these in it, and the answer below shoves him out.
  If BigStanding{} = True
    If MetatileAt{playerX + #PROBE_SIDE_X2, playerY + #PROBE_SIDE_Y_UPPER} <> #MT_EMPTY
      rightSideBlocked = True
    EndIf
    If MetatileAt{playerX + #PROBE_SIDE_X1, playerY + #PROBE_SIDE_Y_UPPER} <> #MT_EMPTY
      leftSideBlocked = True
    EndIf
  EndIf
  ;; Resolve from the probe that is actually blocked. This prevents an
  ;; opposite-facing jump from applying the correction toward the block.
  If rightSideBlocked = True
    If leftSideBlocked = False
      ImpedePlayerMove{-1}
      Statement Return
    EndIf
  EndIf
  If leftSideBlocked = True
    If rightSideBlocked = False
      ImpedePlayerMove{1}
      Statement Return
    EndIf
  EndIf
  ;; If both probes are blocked there is no unique separating side. Retain the
  ;; movement-direction fallback; with zero speed this intentionally chooses
  ;; the rightward correction so the result is deterministic and documented.
  If rightSideBlocked = False Then Statement Return
  If playerSpeedX > 0
    ImpedePlayerMove{-1}
  Else
    ImpedePlayerMove{1}
  EndIf
End Statement

;; The block the head hit. Small Mario cannot break bricks: for him the only block that changes is a
;; question block, which becomes the used block (the hard-block tile, so it lands in the hard-block class).
;; Big Mario breaks a brick instead: CheckPlayerHead{} hands that to BreakBrick{} (sparkydave.debris.blitz.ab3).
;; The ROM searches the nearest metatile; the one probe at the box's centre is enough here.
;;
;; A bump runs clear -> bob -> restore over #MARIO_BLOCKBOUNCE frames: the ROM's animation length and its
;; re-bump lockout. StartBlockBump{} records the cell, its class and GID, clears mapBuffer to sky and queues
;; the cell. DrawBlockBump{} (sparkydave.draw.ab3) draws the block tile as a moving cookie over the cleared cell.
;; metatileBuffer is left alone until the finish, so the cell stays solid and collision is unaffected.
;;
;; FinishBlockBump{} runs the deferred change at the end of the bob: a `?` becomes MT_BLOCK with #SPENT_BLOCK_GID,
;; and a brick gets its saved GID back. The deferral keeps Mario clear of an item rising out of the block.
;; mapBuffer is the source of truth, but nothing re-reads it after StampPlayfield{}, so each change is queued
;; through RedrawMetatileCells{} to be repainted.
;;
;; A `?` releases its item from the generated block-item table (a linear scan is fine: the table is tiny).
;; Several coins can be in flight at once. Only one power-up is out at a time; a new one replaces the old
;; (ClearPowerUps{}). A full pool drops the item.
;;
;; A coin is released on the HIT and pays at once (CoinBlock -> SetupJumpCoin -> GiveOneCoin, smbdis 7307):
;; the arc is only the picture. CheckPlayerHead{} releases the coin kinds; FinishBlockBump{} releases the
;; mushroom, which waits for Mario to clear the cell. A full pool still pays the coin.
;; A brick with something in it (the Starman): listed in the block-item table.
Function.w BrickHoldsItem{bumpCell.w}
  SHARED blockItemCount
  SHARED blockItemCell()
  For itemIndex = 0 To blockItemCount - 1
    If blockItemCell(itemIndex) = bumpCell Then Function Return True
  Next
  Function Return False
End Function

Function.w BlockHoldsCoin{bumpCell.w}
  SHARED blockItemCount
  SHARED blockItemCell(), blockItemSprite()
  For itemIndex = 0 To blockItemCount - 1
    If blockItemCell(itemIndex) = bumpCell
      If blockItemSprite(itemIndex) = #COIN_SPRITE_BASE Then Function Return True
    EndIf
  Next
  Function Return False
End Function

; The coin and its points, now; then the arc if there is a pool slot for it.
Statement PopBlockCoin{coinX.w, coinY.w}
  AddCoin{}
  popSlot.w = PoolAlloc{}
  If popSlot <> -1 Then PoolInitCoin{popSlot, coinX, coinY, coinY}
End Statement

;; The cartridge has a single power-up slot, so a new power-up REPLACES the one that
;; is out (SetupPowerUp rebuilds the object in the same slot, smbdis 7127): the
;; mushroom, flower or star (the 1-UP is a mushroom) still out is simply gone.
Statement ClearPowerUps{}
  SHARED objActive(), objType()
  For clearIndex = 0 To #MAX_OBJECTS - 1
    If objActive(clearIndex) = True
      If objType(clearIndex) = #OBJ_MUSHROOM OR objType(clearIndex) = #OBJ_FLOWER OR objType(clearIndex) = #OBJ_STAR
        PoolFree{clearIndex}
      EndIf
    EndIf
  Next
End Statement

;; THE VINE, grown from the top of a block at baseY in the column at columnX up to targetHeight (the cartridge's Setup_Vine and
;; VineObjectHandler, without the sprites: DrawVine{} draws it). One vine at a time.
Statement StartVine{columnX.w, baseY.w, targetHeight.w}
  SHARED vineColumnX, vineBaseY, vineHeight, vineTarget
  vineColumnX = columnX
  vineBaseY = baseY
  vineHeight = 0
  vineTarget = targetHeight
  SoundSquare2{#SFX_GROW_VINE}
End Statement

Statement SpawnBlockItem{bumpCell.w}
  SHARED blockItemCount
  SHARED blockItemCell(), blockItemSprite()
  SHARED simStepNumerator
  SHARED playerX, playerIsBig
  SHARED objDir(), objType(), objSprite()
  For itemIndex = 0 To blockItemCount - 1
    If blockItemCell(itemIndex) = bumpCell
      itemSprite.w = blockItemSprite(itemIndex)
      bumpX.w = (bumpCell MOD #MAP_W)*#TILE
      bumpY.w = (bumpCell/#MAP_W)*#TILE
      ; Mushroom uses sprite 29/30/31; coin uses #COIN_SPRITE_BASE (42)
      If itemSprite = #COIN_SPRITE_BASE
        PopBlockCoin{bumpX, bumpY - #COIN_H}
        Statement Return
      EndIf
      ; A brick with a vine grows one from its top (Setup_Vine, smbdis 6677): not a power-up, so none is cleared.
      If itemSprite = #VINE_SPRITE
        StartVine{bumpX, bumpY, #VINE_HEIGHT_FULL}
        Statement Return
      EndIf
      ClearPowerUps{}
      slot.w = PoolAlloc{}
      If slot = -1 Then Statement Return
      SoundSquare2{#SFX_GROW_POWER_UP}
      PoolInitBlockItem{slot, bumpX, bumpY, itemSprite, bumpCell, simStepNumerator}
      ;; The cartridge's power-up block gives a big Mario a flower, not a mushroom
      ;; (SetupPowerUp, smbdis 7143). Only the ordinary red mushroom is a power-up;
      ;; the 1-UP is not.
      If itemSprite = #MUSHROOM_SPRITE AND playerIsBig = True
        objType(slot) = #OBJ_FLOWER
        objSprite(slot) = #FLOWER_SPRITE_BASE
      EndIf
      If itemSprite = #STAR_SPRITE_BASE Then objType(slot) = #OBJ_STAR
      ; In SMB 1 the mushroom always moves to the right initially.
      If #ITEM_FIXED_DIRECTION = 1
        objDir(slot) = #MARIO_BTN_RIGHT
      Else
        If playerX < bumpX
          objDir(slot) = #MARIO_BTN_RIGHT
        Else
          objDir(slot) = #MARIO_BTN_LEFT
        EndIf
      EndIf
      Statement Return
    EndIf
  Next
End Statement

;; Floating coins are tile #COIN_TILE_GID in mapBuffer (authored in the
;; Interactive Layer), not objects, and not solid: only the art is there. Two
;; ways to collect one, both clearing the cell to sky:
;;   - from below: a block bumped or broken with a coin directly above it pops
;;     that coin out as if the block had released it (CheckTopOfBlock, smbdis
;;     7396, then SetupJumpCoin). The same coin object as a block coin, and like
;;     one it pays at once: PopBlockCoin{} adds the coin and 200 points and the arc
;;     is only the picture.
;;   - by touch: any of the player's five tile probes lying in a coin cell.
;;     The ROM reaches coins through those same probes (CheckForCoinMTiles), so a
;;     coin is collected when a probe, not the whole sprite, reaches it. The
;;     coin and 200 points land at once.
;; The cleared cell becomes plain sky: map art behind a coin is not restored. The
;; back playfield's scenery (the strip) is not in the map, so it shows through.
Statement ClearTileCoin{coinCell.w}
  SHARED mapBuffer()
  mapBuffer(coinCell) = 0
  QueueMetatileRedraw{coinCell}
End Statement

Statement CollectCoinAbove{blockCellIndex.w}
  SHARED mapBuffer(), mapWidth
  If blockCellIndex < mapWidth Then Statement Return
  coinCell.w = blockCellIndex - mapWidth
  If mapBuffer(coinCell) <> #COIN_TILE_GID Then Statement Return
  ClearTileCoin{coinCell}
  PopBlockCoin{(coinCell MOD mapWidth)*#TILE, (coinCell/mapWidth)*#TILE}
End Statement

Statement CollectCoinAt{pixelX.w, pixelY.w}
  SHARED mapBuffer(), mapWidth, areaColumns
  If pixelX < 0 Then Statement Return
  If pixelY < 0 Then Statement Return
  tileColumn.w = pixelX/#TILE
  tileRow.w = pixelY/#TILE
  If tileColumn >= areaColumns Then Statement Return
  If tileRow >= #MAP_H Then Statement Return
  coinCell.w = tileRow*mapWidth + tileColumn
  If mapBuffer(coinCell) <> #COIN_TILE_GID Then Statement Return
  ClearTileCoin{coinCell}
  AddCoin{}
End Statement

Statement CheckPlayerCoins{}
  SHARED playerX, playerY, playerIsBig
  headY.w = #PROBE_TOP_Y
  If playerIsBig = True Then headY = #PROBE_TOP_Y_BIG
  CollectCoinAt{playerX + #PROBE_TOP_X, playerY + headY}
  CollectCoinAt{playerX + #PROBE_FOOT_X1, playerY + #PROBE_FOOT_Y}
  CollectCoinAt{playerX + #PROBE_FOOT_X2, playerY + #PROBE_FOOT_Y}
  CollectCoinAt{playerX + #PROBE_SIDE_X1, playerY + #PROBE_SIDE_Y}
  CollectCoinAt{playerX + #PROBE_SIDE_X2, playerY + #PROBE_SIDE_Y}
End Statement

;; The brick with coins (the cartridge's $58 and $5d, smbdis 7226-7262, brought in
;; by a `multicoin` BlockItem in the level, which becomes multiCoinCell()). It
;; bumps for BOTH sizes, so big Mario cannot break it, and every hit pays a coin
;; and keeps the brick, until the timer runs out. The first hit starts the timer
;; at #MULTI_COIN_INTERVALS; it counts down one interval every #INTERVAL_PERIOD
;; ticks of a free-running phase counter, as the cartridge's interval timers do
;; (IntervalTimerControl, smbdis 790-793), so the brick lasts from 10 * #INTERVAL_PERIOD + 1 to 11 * #INTERVAL_PERIOD ticks
;; from the first hit (211 to 231 on NTSC), depending on where in the phase it landed. The hit
;; after the timer has reached 0 still pays its coin, but that bump settles as
;; the used block instead of the brick.
Function.w MultiCoinIndex{cellIndex.w}
  SHARED multiCoinCount
  SHARED multiCoinCell()
  For multiIndex = 0 To multiCoinCount - 1
    If multiCoinCell(multiIndex) = cellIndex Then Function Return multiIndex
  Next
  Function Return -1
End Function

; Called on the hit, after StartBlockBump{}.
Statement HitMultiCoinBrick{multiIndex.w, cellIndex.w}
  SHARED multiCoinStarted(), multiCoinIntervals(), blockBumpExhausts
  If multiCoinStarted(multiIndex) = False
    multiCoinStarted(multiIndex) = True
    multiCoinIntervals(multiIndex) = #MULTI_COIN_INTERVALS
  EndIf
  blockBumpExhausts = False
  If multiCoinIntervals(multiIndex) = 0 Then blockBumpExhausts = True
  PopBlockCoin{(cellIndex MOD #MAP_W)*#TILE, (cellIndex/#MAP_W)*#TILE - #COIN_H}
End Statement

; Once per sim tick, from timing.ab3.
Statement UpdateIntervalTimers{}
  SHARED multiCoinStarted(), multiCoinIntervals(), intervalPhase, playerInjuryIntervals, playerState
  SHARED objActive(), objType(), objState(), objAux(), playerStarIntervals, worldFreezeTicks, objPopTimer(), castleEndTask
  intervalPhase = intervalPhase - 1
  If intervalPhase >= 0 Then Statement Return
  intervalPhase = #INTERVAL_PERIOD - 1
  ; Not during a grow, a hit or a flower: the cartridge halts its timers for the freeze (TimerControl).
  If worldFreezeTicks = 0
    If playerInjuryIntervals > 0 Then playerInjuryIntervals = playerInjuryIntervals - 1
    If playerStarIntervals > 0 Then playerStarIntervals = playerStarIntervals - 1
    ;; The star's music ends a little before the star does (smbdis 5333: at 4 left),
    ;; and the area's own music comes back. Not from the axe on: that check is in the
    ;; cartridge's GameCoreRoutine, which its victory mode does not run, so a star running
    ;; out on the bridge or the walk does not bring the castle's music back.
    If playerStarIntervals = 4 AND playerState <> #PLAYER_ST_AXE AND castleEndTask = 0 Then QueueAreaMusic{}
    ;; What waits in intervals: a stomped Koopa's shell (its revive) and a squashed Goomba (its
    ;; removal, ChkKillGoomba). Each keeps its count in objAux.
    For poolIndex = 0 To #MAX_OBJECTS - 1
      If objActive(poolIndex) = True AND ((objType(poolIndex) = #OBJ_KOOPA AND objState(poolIndex) = #KOOPA_ST_SHELL) OR (objType(poolIndex) = #OBJ_GOOMBA AND objState(poolIndex) = #GOOMBA_ST_SQUASHED))
        If objAux(poolIndex) > 0 Then objAux(poolIndex) = objAux(poolIndex) - 1
      EndIf
      ; A Bloober's float down and a Podoboo's wait (their EnemyIntervalTimer).
      If objActive(poolIndex) = True AND (objType(poolIndex) = #OBJ_BLOOBER OR objType(poolIndex) = #OBJ_PODOBOO)
        If objPopTimer(poolIndex) > 0 Then objPopTimer(poolIndex) = objPopTimer(poolIndex) - 1
      EndIf
    Next
  EndIf
  For multiIndex = 0 To #MAX_MULTI_COINS - 1
    If multiCoinStarted(multiIndex) = True
      If multiCoinIntervals(multiIndex) > 0 Then multiCoinIntervals(multiIndex) = multiCoinIntervals(multiIndex) - 1
    EndIf
  Next
End Statement

Statement StartBlockBump{blockCell.w, blockClass.w, blockGid.w}
  SHARED mapBuffer(), blockBumpActive, blockBumpCell, blockBumpClass, blockBumpGid
  SHARED blockBumpExhausts, bumpObjectCell
  bumpObjectCell = blockCell
  blockBumpExhausts = False
  blockBumpCell = blockCell
  blockBumpClass = blockClass
  blockBumpGid = blockGid
  blockBumpActive = True
  SoundSquare1{#SFX_BUMP}
  ;; Clear the cell to sky and queue it once: by the time the bob draws it,
  ;; the 2-frame drain has made it sky in both bitmaps, so the bob always
  ;; covers a cleared cell and never a double image.
  mapBuffer(blockCell) = 0
  QueueMetatileRedraw{blockCell}
End Statement

Statement FinishBlockBump{}
  SHARED metatileBuffer(), mapBuffer(), blockBumpCell, blockBumpClass, blockBumpGid
  SHARED blockBumpActive, blockBumpExhausts
  If blockBumpClass = #MT_QUESTION OR blockBumpExhausts = True
    metatileBuffer(blockBumpCell) = #MT_BLOCK
    mapBuffer(blockBumpCell) = #SPENT_BLOCK_GID
    QueueMetatileRedraw{blockBumpCell}
    ;; Coins were released on the hit; only a mushroom, flower or star waits for the
    ;; settle. A brick that is not in the table releases nothing.
    If BlockHoldsCoin{blockBumpCell} = False Then SpawnBlockItem{blockBumpCell}
  Else
    ;; Brick: the class never changes; restore the original art that
    ;; StartBlockBump{} saved. A big Mario never gets here for a plain brick: he
    ;; breaks it in CheckPlayerHead{} through BreakBrick{}. A multi-coin brick
    ;; does, and restores here until its timer has run out.
    mapBuffer(blockBumpCell) = blockBumpGid
    QueueMetatileRedraw{blockBumpCell}
  EndIf
  blockBumpActive = False
End Statement

Statement UpdateBlockBump{}
  SHARED blockBumpActive, blockBounceTimer
  If blockBumpActive = False Then Statement Return
  ;; The caller decremented blockBounceTimer just before calling: it reaches
  ;; 0 exactly once, so the finalize fires exactly once.
  If blockBounceTimer = 0 Then FinishBlockBump{}
End Statement

;; The hidden 1-UP block (the cartridge's Hidden1UpBlock, smbdis 4172, and metatile
;; $60, which every probe treats as empty, ChkInvisibleMTiles 12238). The cell is
;; sky in the map, drawn as nothing and not solid, so Mario passes through it and
;; lands on nothing; only a head hit from below finds it. That makes it the used
;; block and releases the item, exactly as a `?` block does, so CheckPlayerHead{}
;; turns the cell into MT_QUESTION for the hit and the rest is the `?` path.
;;
;; The block is only there if Hidden1UpFlag was set when the level decoder built
;; it, and building it clears the flag: ActivateHiddenBlocks{} does that when the
;; screen reaches the cell, and a block already behind the screen is not built, so
;; a restart at the halfway point leaves the flag for a block still ahead.
;; #HIDDEN_ONEUP_ONCE = 0 sets the flag again on every restart.
Function.w HiddenBlockIndex{cellIndex.w}
  SHARED hiddenBlockCount
  SHARED hiddenBlockCell()
  For hiddenIndex = 0 To hiddenBlockCount - 1
    If hiddenBlockCell(hiddenIndex) = cellIndex Then Function Return hiddenIndex
  Next
  Function Return -1
End Function

;; Once per sim tick, with the Goomba wake.
;; A hidden COIN block (2-1's two; the cartridge's metatile $5f, a coin from a block nothing shows) is the same cell without the flag: it is
;; always built, as the level decoder has no flag for it, and pays its coin on the hit through the `?` path (BlockHoldsCoin{}).
Statement ActivateHiddenBlocks{}
  SHARED hiddenBlockCount
  SHARED hiddenBlockCell(), hiddenBlockLive(), hiddenOneUpFlag, cameraX
  For hiddenIndex = 0 To hiddenBlockCount - 1
    If hiddenBlockLive(hiddenIndex) = False
      ; In the view first: the lookup for the coin block is only made for the one the screen has reached.
      hiddenX.w = (hiddenBlockCell(hiddenIndex) MOD #MAP_W)*#TILE
      If hiddenX + #TILE > cameraX AND hiddenX <= cameraX + #VIEW_W
        isCoinBlock.w = BlockHoldsCoin{hiddenBlockCell(hiddenIndex)}
        If hiddenOneUpFlag = True OR isCoinBlock = True
          hiddenBlockLive(hiddenIndex) = True
          If #HIDDEN_ONEUP_ONCE = 1 AND isCoinBlock = False Then hiddenOneUpFlag = False
        EndIf
      EndIf
    EndIf
  Next
End Statement

Statement CheckPlayerHead{}
  SHARED playerX, playerY, playerSpeedY, blockBounceTimer, metatileBuffer(), mapBuffer(), mapWidth
  SHARED playerIsBig, headProbeY, hiddenBlockLive(), blockBumpExhausts, currentAreaType
  If playerSpeedY >= 0 Then Statement Return
  ;; The head gate: nothing happens in the top 16 px of the world (#MT_HEAD_MIN_Y), and no hit until Mario
  ;; has risen past the first 4 px of the tile (#MT_HEAD_MIN_SUBTILE), so he clips into a ceiling first.
  If playerY < #MT_HEAD_MIN_Y Then Statement Return
  If (playerY AND $0f) < #MT_HEAD_MIN_SUBTILE Then Statement Return
  ;; The one size-dependent probe. Big Mario's head is 14 px higher; his feet
  ;; and his 12-px width are the same as small, so nothing else moves. That is
  ;; what makes a grown Mario able to stand where a small one cannot. A crouching big Mario has
  ;; the small head probe (ChkCollSize, smbdis 11931).
  If BigStanding{} = True
    headProbeY.w = #PROBE_TOP_Y_BIG
    If currentAreaType = #AREA_TYPE_WATER Then headProbeY = #PROBE_TOP_Y_BIG_SWIM
  Else
    headProbeY.w = #PROBE_TOP_Y
  EndIf
  blockClass.w = MetatileAt{playerX + #PROBE_TOP_X, playerY + headProbeY}
  ;; In water (PlayerBGCollision, 11950 to 11973) no block is bumped or broken: a solid metatile plays the bump, and either
  ;; way the rise stops. The NTSC cartridge sets speed 1 (down) here; both builds take the PAL listing's fix, 0 in water
  ;; (its NYSpd, 11959).
  If currentAreaType = #AREA_TYPE_WATER
    If blockClass = #MT_EMPTY Then Statement Return
    If blockClass <> #MT_BRICK AND blockClass <> #MT_QUESTION Then SoundSquare1{#SFX_BUMP}
    playerSpeedY = 0
    Statement Return
  EndIf
  headCell.w = (playerY + headProbeY)/#TILE*mapWidth + (playerX + #PROBE_TOP_X)/#TILE
  bumpGid.w = -1
  If blockClass = #MT_EMPTY
    ;; Nothing there, unless it is a hidden block that has been built and not yet
    ;; hit. It becomes solid for the hit and bobs as the used block.
    If blockBounceTimer <> 0 Then Statement Return
    hiddenIndex.w = HiddenBlockIndex{headCell}
    If hiddenIndex < 0 Then Statement Return
    If hiddenBlockLive(hiddenIndex) = False Then Statement Return
    metatileBuffer(headCell) = #MT_QUESTION
    blockClass = #MT_QUESTION
    bumpGid = #SPENT_BLOCK_GID
  EndIf
  If blockClass = #MT_BRICK OR blockClass = #MT_QUESTION
    ;; Not a solid metatile, so the ROM takes the block-object path rather
    ;; than the hard bump, and BumpBlock clears the vertical speed outright
    ;; (7314). The block keeps its own 16-frame bounce lockout so a held
    ;; button cannot re-trigger it (BlockBounceTimer, 11962-11963 and 7264);
    ;; the same counter clocks the bump animation, so one bump at a time.
    playerSpeedY = 0
    If blockBounceTimer = 0
      bumpCell.w = headCell
      ; Bump or break, a coin sitting on the block is collected (CheckTopOfBlock).
      CollectCoinAbove{bumpCell}
      multiIndex.w = -1
      If blockClass = #MT_BRICK Then multiIndex = MultiCoinIndex{bumpCell}
      itemBrick.w = False
      If blockClass = #MT_BRICK Then itemBrick = BrickHoldsItem{bumpCell}
      If blockClass = #MT_BRICK AND playerIsBig = True AND multiIndex < 0 AND itemBrick = False
        ;; Big Mario breaks it (BrickShatter, smbdis 7379): no bob, the cell
        ;; empties at once, and his vertical speed becomes -2 rather than 0 so he
        ;; keeps rising through the gap, and the lockout is armed. A break never
        ;; refuses: see the two chunk sets in sparkydave.debris.blitz.ab3.
        BreakBrick{bumpCell}
        playerSpeedY = #BRICK_BREAK_SPEED_Y
        blockBounceTimer = #MARIO_BLOCKBOUNCE
      Else
        If bumpGid < 0 Then bumpGid = mapBuffer(bumpCell)
        StartBlockBump{bumpCell, blockClass, bumpGid}
        blockBounceTimer = #MARIO_BLOCKBOUNCE
        If multiIndex >= 0 Then HitMultiCoinBrick{multiIndex, bumpCell}
        ; A brick with an item bumps for either size, and is the used block after.
        If itemBrick = True Then blockBumpExhausts = True
        ; A `?` block's coin pays on the hit; its mushroom waits for the settle.
        If blockClass = #MT_QUESTION
          If BlockHoldsCoin{bumpCell} = True Then SpawnBlockItem{bumpCell}
        EndIf
      EndIf
    EndIf
  Else
    ;; Hard terrain, the solid-metatile path: downward speed 1, subpixel
    ;; untouched (NYSpd, 11972-11973). No position change, like the ROM.
    playerSpeedY = 1
  EndIf
End Statement

;; The player's box, for object collision: moving items, Goombas and Koopas read it. Small Mario is 10x12 at
;; (+3, +4) in the 16-px sprite; big is 12x24 at (+2, +8) of the 32-px one, and crouching is 12x12 at (+2, +20).
;; The two size states differ only in the numbers fed in, so this is the size branch rather than a second box.
;;
;; Crouching has big's width and the small box's height.
Statement UpdatePlayerBoundBox{}
  SHARED playerX, playerY, playerIsBig
  SHARED boundBoxLeft, boundBoxTop, boundBoxRight, boundBoxBottom
  If playerIsBig = True
    boundBoxLeft = playerX + #BOUND_LEFT_BIG
    boundBoxTop = playerY + #BOUND_TOP_BIG
    boundBoxRight = playerX + #BOUND_RIGHT_BIG
    boundBoxBottom = playerY + #BOUND_BOTTOM_BIG
    ; Crouching: big's 12 px width and small's height (12 x 12 at (+2, +20) of the 32 px sprite).
    If BigStanding{} = False Then boundBoxTop = playerY + #BOUND_TOP
  Else
    boundBoxLeft = playerX + #BOUND_LEFT
    boundBoxTop = playerY + #BOUND_TOP
    boundBoxRight = playerX + #BOUND_RIGHT
    boundBoxBottom = playerY + #BOUND_BOTTOM
  EndIf
End Statement

;; END OF COLLISION (BLITZ MODE)
